              <a href="#application" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">6.</span><span>Application</span></a>
              <a href="#traction-heat" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">7.</span><span>Traction and Heat</span></a>
              <a href="#coverage-cost" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">8.</span><span>Coverage and Cost</span></a>
              <a href="#upkeep" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">9.</span><span>Upkeep and Exit Plan</span></a>
              <a href="#alternatives" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">10.</span><span>When to Skip Paint</span></a>
              <a href="#mistakes" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">11.</span><span>Common Mistakes</span></a>
              <a href="#faq" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">12.</span><span>FAQ</span></a>
        </nav>
      </details>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Deck paint has a bad reputation, and some of it is earned. Horizontal wood is the hardest surface on a house to keep coated: it bakes in sun, holds standing water, flexes under feet and soaks up moisture from below. A film that would last a decade on siding can lift in two seasons on a deck. That does not mean paint is a mistake. It means the decision should start with the deck rather than the can. A structurally sound deck with weathered, mismatched or previously coated boards is a good candidate. A deck with soft spots, chronic dampness or boards that are still shedding old stain is not, and no product on this list will change that.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 6 Best Deck Paints, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Picks are ranked using manufacturer data sheets, label coverage and recoat guidance, published warranty terms, and patterns in owner feedback over several seasons. We did not run our own exposure panels. Each product is matched to the deck condition it suits, because the right coating for a sound deck is the wrong one for a cracked one.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="triage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Should You Paint This Deck? A Five-Minute Triage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with a screwdriver, not a paint chip. Push it into the boards near the house, around posts, on the stair treads and anywhere water pools. If it sinks in more than about an eighth of an inch with light pressure, that wood is soft or rotting. Coating it hides the problem for a season while the rot keeps spreading underneath. Replace soft boards first, and check the joists below them while they are open.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, look at the existing finish. If water beads on the boards, a sealer or stain is still active and paint will not bond to it. If the old coating is peeling in sheets, everything loose must come off before anything new goes on. If the boards are bare and gray, they are the easiest to paint after a thorough cleaning and a light sanding to remove loose fibers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then check moisture and airflow. Decks built low to the ground, over grass or wet soil, or with little gap between boards stay damp from below. Paint traps that moisture, and the coating bubbles and lifts from the underside. A pin moisture meter reading in the low to mid teens is a reasonable target before painting. A deck that reads higher than that on a dry week is a poor candidate for any film.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, be honest about what you want. Paint and solid stain are the right answer when you want an even color, to hide mismatched replacement boards, or to cover stains and graying that cleaning cannot fix. They are the wrong answer if you love the grain, because once a film goes on, getting back to bare wood means stripping and sanding every board.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
