        <div id="three-coatings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Solid Stain, Floor Paint and Resurfacer: Three Different Products
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Solid color deck stain</strong> is the closest thing to paint that still behaves like a stain. It is usually an acrylic or acrylic-modified coating that hides the grain but stays thinner and more flexible than paint, so it follows the texture of the board. Most solid stains are designed to be recoated without stripping as long as the surface is sound. For bare or recently stripped decks in full sun, solid stain is the default recommendation.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Porch and floor paint</strong> is a true paint, usually a one-part acrylic latex enamel formulated to resist scuffing. It forms a smoother, slightly harder film than solid stain and shines on covered porches, screened rooms and decks that see moderate sun. On open decks in harsh climates, its harder film is more prone to cracking with the wood's movement, so it suits protected surfaces best.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Resurfacers</strong> are thick-bodied coatings, often built up to many times the thickness of normal paint. They fill checks and small cracks, lock down splinters and leave a textured surface with better traction. They are meant for decks that are structurally fine but cosmetically worn out. They also have the most complicated history: resurfacers as a category, including early formulas from major brands, drew class-action complaints over peeling, and their success depends heavily on dry wood and careful prep.
          </p>

          <p>
            These products are not interchangeable, and mixing them causes trouble. A resurfacer over a peeling solid stain inherits that stain's weak bond. A porch paint over a resurfacer can look fine and then crack as the thick layer beneath moves. Choose one system for the whole deck and stick with the same family on recoats, using the primer the manufacturer specifies if there is one.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you only want to change the color of a deck that still has sound grain and you are willing to recoat every few years, look first at a semi-transparent product in our <a href="/best-deck-stain-reviews/">deck stain rankings</a>. It will never peel the way a film can, and it keeps the option of going lighter or back to natural later.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-it-peels" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Why Deck Paint Peels, and How to Stop It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Nearly every peeling deck traces back to moisture behind the film. Wood swells as it absorbs water and shrinks as it dries. A paint film has some flexibility but far less than the wood beneath it. When water gets in through a crack, an unsealed end, a nail hole or the underside of the board, the film is pushed off from behind. Sunlight then heats and softens the coating and finishes the job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coat every face you can reach. On new or replaced boards, prime or seal all four sides and both ends before installing them, or at least the ends. On existing decks, coat the board edges and ends, not just the top face. Water entering through uncoated ends is one of the most common causes of peeling that starts at the board tips and spreads inward.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, do not apply film over a surface that will not hold it. Old penetrating stain, waxy water repellents, mill glaze on new lumber and chalky residue from failed coatings all prevent adhesion. The tape test is simple: press masking tape firmly onto an area you have prepped and coated, let it dry, then pull it off quickly. If paint comes away on the tape, the surface needs more preparation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thickness is the last factor. Two thin coats outperform one heavy coat for everything but resurfacers, which are designed to go on thick but still within the label's limits. Overbuilt paint cracks as it ages, and cracks invite water. Measure out how much product a section should take and stick to it.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Paint fails from behind, not from on top.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Most peeling starts where water gets under the film through ends, edges, cracks or the underside of the board. Seal those paths, paint only dry wood, and the coating has a real chance of lasting.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Prep and Priming: Most of the Job
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean first. A deck cleaner and a scrub brush remove dirt, mildew and old chalking finish. Rinse with a pressure washer on a wide fan setting at low pressure, keeping the tip well back from the wood and moving with the grain. Our pressure washer guide, <a href="/best-pressure-washer/">best pressure washers</a>, covers models that adjust down far enough for softwood.
          </p>

          <p>
            Remove anything that is not firmly bonded. A deck stripper handles old solid stain and loose paint across a whole surface; a scraper and a sander handle smaller areas. Sand with roughly 60 to 80 grit to open the wood and knock down raised fibers, then sweep and vacuum the dust out of the board gaps. A deck that has been stripped also needs a brightener to neutralize the stripper and restore the wood's pH.
          </p>

          <p>
            Repair before you coat. Set any popped nails or replace them with deck screws, swap out cracked or soft boards, and fill small holes in railings with an exterior filler. On walking surfaces, fillers tend to pop out as the wood moves, so replacing a badly split board is the more durable fix. Our <a href="/best-wood-filler/">wood filler guide</a> covers products that hold up outdoors on railings and trim.
          </p>

          <p>
            Let it dry. After washing or stripping, most coating labels want at least 48 hours of dry weather, and new pressure-treated lumber may need weeks or months before it accepts any film. If you are working with new treated boards, our guide on whether <a href="/can-you-stain-pressure-treated-wood/">you can stain pressure-treated wood</a> walks through the absorption test that tells you when it is ready.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prime when the label calls for it. Many solid stains and resurfacers are self-priming on bare wood, while porch paints often call for an exterior primer. Cedar and redwood bleed tannins that can discolor light paint, so a stain-blocking exterior primer is worth using under light colors on those species. Use the primer the topcoat manufacturer names when one is listed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Applying Deck Paint for a Finish That Lasts
          </h2>

          <p>
            Check the label's temperature window for both the air and the wood. Most acrylic coatings want at least 50 degrees, sometimes higher, and need to stay above that through the night. Avoid painting in direct midday sun, which dries the surface before the film levels, and avoid evenings when dew will form before the coating sets.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cut in the edges and gaps with a brush first, then roll the board faces. Work two or three boards at a time along their full length so you always roll into wet paint. A brush or a thin putty knife pushed between boards clears paint that bridges the gap, which otherwise traps water and leaves a line that cracks.
          </p>

          <p>
            For resurfacers, use the roller nap and spread rate the manufacturer specifies. These products depend on a specific thickness, and the most common mistake is spreading them too thin to stretch the gallon or too thick to fill a crack in one pass. Fill larger cracks separately with the manufacturer's recommended filler or a first spot coat, then apply the full coat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Apply two coats unless the label says otherwise, waiting the full recoat time between them. On steps and the main traffic paths, a third coat or an anti-slip additive is a worthwhile upgrade. Do railings and vertical parts first, then the floor, and finish at the exit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Respect cure time. Many coatings are dry to the touch in a few hours but take several days to harden fully. Light foot traffic is usually allowed after a day; furniture, grills and planters should wait longer, and rugs should stay off for at least a week so they do not trap moisture or stick to the film.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="traction-heat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Traction, Heat and Color Choice
          </h2>

          <p>
            A smooth painted deck is noticeably more slippery when wet than bare wood. For stairs, pool surrounds and shaded decks that hold dew, choose a textured coating or add a fine anti-slip aggregate to the final coat. Most paint makers sell a compatible additive. Mix it thoroughly and stir often, because it settles to the bottom of the tray.
          </p>

          <p>
            Color matters for comfort as much as appearance. Dark solid colors on a south or west facing deck can get uncomfortably hot under bare feet in summer, and darker films also expand and contract more through daily temperature swings. Mid-tone grays, tans and browns are more forgiving on both counts and hide dirt and pollen better than very light colors.
          </p>

          <p>
            Light colors show every leaf stain and footprint, and on cedar or redwood they can turn yellow or tan as tannins bleed through. If you want a pale deck, use a stain-blocking primer and plan on more frequent cleaning. Many owners split the difference with a darker floor and lighter railings, which also hides wear on the walking surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before committing, paint a sample on a scrap board or an out-of-the-way stair tread and look at it in morning and afternoon light. Solid stains and floor paints often dry a shade different from the chip. A one-quart test costs far less than repainting a whole deck in a color you dislike.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Rules of thumb</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2 coats</p>
