        <div id="coverage-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Coverage and Cost
          </h2>

          <p>
            Coverage varies more across deck coatings than across almost any other paint category. Standard porch paints and solid stains often list roughly 250 to 400 square feet per gallon per coat on smooth surfaces. Resurfacers may list as little as 50 to 75 square feet per gallon per coat because they go on so thick. Read the label for the specific product before you budget.
          </p>

          <p>
            Measure the deck floor, then add railings and stairs. Rails with pickets can add nearly half again to the floor area, and each stair adds tread, riser and stringer faces. Use the rough-surface coverage figure for weathered or stripped wood and plan for two coats. Buying all the product at once from the same batch also avoids slight color differences between cans.
          </p>

          <p>
            At the time of writing, quality solid stains and porch paints were roughly 40 to 70 dollars per gallon, and resurfacers were often 45 to 70 dollars per gallon with far lower coverage. For a 300 square foot deck, that can mean roughly 150 to 300 dollars in solid stain or porch paint, compared with several hundred dollars or more for a resurfacer, before primer, stripper and tools.
          </p>

          <p>
            The hidden cost is time. Stripping, sanding and repairing a neglected deck can take a weekend or more by itself, before any coating goes on. If you are hiring out, ask whether the quote includes stripping and board replacement, since those line items are where estimates diverge. Cost per year of service is a better comparison than price per gallon.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compare the coating budget to replacement as well. If more than about a quarter of the boards are cracked, cupped or soft, new decking over sound framing may cost less over the next decade than repeated resurfacing. Resurfacers are worth the money when the structure is good and the problem is mostly cosmetic.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="upkeep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Upkeep and the Exit Plan
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wash a painted deck once or twice a year with a mild deck cleaner and a soft brush. Mildew and dirt hold moisture against the film and dull the color. Avoid high pressure, which can lift coating at worn edges. Clear leaves and debris from board gaps so water drains instead of soaking into edges.
          </p>

          <p>
            Touch up early. Small chips at stair noses and in the main traffic lane are normal. Sand the edges of the chip, spot prime if bare wood shows, and apply thin coats of the same product. Catching wear early keeps water from creeping under the film and turning a small spot into a peeling patch.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expect to recoat solid stain and porch paint every few years in full sun and less often under cover. Clean and lightly scuff the surface first, and use the same product family. Resurfacers are usually recoated with the same resurfacer, but read the label; some limit how many layers can be stacked before the coating must come off.
          </p>

          <p>
            Know your exit plan before you start. Removing a solid coating from a deck means chemical stripping and heavy sanding, and resurfacers are especially stubborn. If you might want the natural wood back someday, a semi-transparent stain is the better choice now. Clear and lightly tinted options are covered in our <a href="/best-deck-sealant/">deck sealant guide</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep records. Write the product name, color, batch number and date on the inside of the garage door or in a phone note. When it is time to touch up, matching the exact product avoids adhesion problems and a patchwork of slightly different colors.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. When Paint Is the Wrong Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip paint on decks in constant shade with poor airflow. They stay damp, grow mildew under and on the film, and peel early. Improving drainage, trimming overhanging branches and using a penetrating finish usually works better on these decks than any coating.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip paint on new cedar, redwood or tropical hardwoods unless you have a specific reason. Those woods were chosen for their grain and natural durability, and covering them wastes that. A toned penetrating finish keeps them looking like what they are; our <a href="/best-redwood-sealer/">redwood sealer guide</a> covers finishes that preserve the warm color.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip paint on decks you are not prepared to maintain. A film neglected for several years fails in patches and is harder to restore than a faded stain. If you know the deck will be ignored, a semi-transparent penetrating stain degrades more gracefully and is easier to bring back.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip resurfacers on decks that move. Loose framing, bouncy boards and large gaps will crack a thick coating. Tighten or add framing, secure boards with screws, and only then decide whether a resurfacer is needed.
          </p>

          <p>
            And skip paint altogether on composite decking unless the board maker publishes a coating system for it. Most composite manufacturers do not recommend coating their boards at all; a cleaning is usually all they need.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting over a finish that still repels water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If water beads on the boards, an old sealer or penetrating stain is still active and paint will not bond to it. The new coating may look fine for weeks and then peel in sheets. Strip the old finish or let it weather fully, clean and lightly sand, and test adhesion with tape on a small area first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating a deck that stays damp</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Low decks over wet ground, decks under trees and boards with no gap between them hold moisture from below. A film traps that water, and the coating blisters and lifts from the underside. Fix drainage and airflow, check readings with a moisture meter, and consider a penetrating finish instead of paint on chronically damp decks.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spreading a resurfacer too thin</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Resurfacers rely on a specific thickness to fill cracks and resist wear. Stretching a gallon to cover more area leaves a thin, weak layer that wears through quickly and fails to bridge checks. Use the roller and spread rate the label specifies, fill larger cracks separately and buy enough product for two full coats.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving board ends and edges bare</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water that enters through uncoated ends and edges gets behind the film and pushes it off, starting at the board tips. Brush coating into the ends, sides and gaps of every board, not just the top face. On new or replacement boards, coat the ends before installation so the most absorbent grain is protected.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a dark color for a sunny deck</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Very dark solid colors on decks facing south or west can become uncomfortably hot underfoot and expand and contract more each day, which stresses the film. Mid-tone colors stay cooler, hide dirt and pollen better and tend to last longer. Test a sample board in full sun before committing.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

